{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2501.14756, what are two overlapping information requirements between DPIA and FRIA, and what is one requirement unique to FRIA?", "answer": "Both require systematic description and risk mitigation measures; FRIA uniquely requires provenance of the AI system.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "Based on this, we establish the following information requirements as inputs to the DPIA process:"}, {"page": 9, "quote": "Further, the AI Act has more information requirements which do not correspond to requirements for the DPIA - such as the specific use of data in training and validation, user interfaces to manage the AI systems and explain its outputs, provenance of the AI system and its development, and planned changes to the AI system."}]},
  {"question": "What are the four broad categories of information for FRIA identified in arXiv 2501.14756?", "answer": "Systematic description, proportionality and necessity assessment, risks to rights, and risk mitigation.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "In Section 3.2 we identified the information required in the FRIA process, and in Section 3.4 we organised them in 4 broad categories as: systematic description, proportionality and necessity assessment, risks to rights, and risk mitigation."}]},
  {"question": "How does arXiv 2501.14756 define 'necessity' in the context of DPIA under GDPR?", "answer": "Necessity is a combined, fact-based assessment of effectiveness and whether the measure is less intrusive compared to other options.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "Necessity of processing and data e.g. as a ' statutory or contractual requirement' (Art.13-3e), which is defined by the EDPS as \" a combined, fact-based assessment of the effectiveness of the measure for the objective pursued and of whether it is less intrusive compared to other options for achieving the same goal\" [18];"}]},
  {"question": "What is the role of the AI Office regarding FRIA tools as described in arXiv 2501.14756?", "answer": "The AI Office must develop a questionnaire and automated tool to facilitate deployers in complying with obligations in a simplified manner.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "The AI Act Art.27-5 requires the AI Office to develop a questionnaire and an automated tool to \"to facilitate deployers in complying with their obligations ... in a simplified manner \" . Recital 96 further clarifies that this requires the AI Office to \"develop a template for a questionnaire in order to facilitate compliance and reduce the administrative burden for deployers \""}]} ,
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2501.14756, what is required to assess whether a FRIA is necessary?", "answer": "Information about the AI system's role, high-risk categorisation, and any exceptions or exemptions must be considered.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "In this stage, The tool takes as input information regarding the AI system being assessed, such as whether it is being developed and put on the market and/or it is being deployed, whether it satisfies any high-risk criteria established in Art.6 or Art.27, and whether it is subject to any exception or exemption regarding the classification of high-risk and the requirement for a FRIA."}]},
  {"question": "What does arXiv 2501.14756 say about the process for determining if a DPIA is required when using new technologies?", "answer": "GDPR requires a DPIA when using new technologies, especially AI, or when automated processing produces significant effects.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "The GDPR states that assessing whether a DPIA is required should be done \"in particular (when) using new technologies \" - which Artificial Intelligence (AI) technologies are. The GDPR also requires a DPIA where automated processing produces 'legal or similarly significant effects ' (Art.35-3a), where large scale special categories of personal data are involved (Art.35-3b), and where there is a systematic large scale monitoring of a public area (Art.35-3c)."}]}
]}
